 **The Android status bar (2026-09-26, owner decision, replacing the phase-1 assumption).** An installed Chrome app (a WebAPK) takes the status bar's **colour** from the manifest snapshot baked at install and ignores the page's `theme-color` meta; the **icon colour follows the phone's own light/dark setting**. The phase-1 manifest declared `theme_color` #0b0b0c on the assumption that Chrome picks the icons from that colour's luminance; on the S23 it does not, so a phone in light mode showed a black bar with dark icons (the clock unreadable). One manifest colour cannot be readable in both phone modes, and Chrome has no dependable per-scheme manifest colour, so **the manifest declares no `theme_color`**: Chrome uses the platform bar, white with dark icons in light mode and black with light icons in dark mode. `background_color` stays #0b0b0c, so **the splash stays dark**. **Accepted trade-off:** with the phone in light mode, a white status bar sits above the dark splash for about half a second. The page's `theme-color` meta still follows the app's theme for a browser tab's toolbar (`core/ui/theme/theme-color.ts`). Pinned in `pwa-files.test.ts`; after a manifest change an installed app picks it up when Chrome refreshes its WebAPK, or at once on reinstall.
 
